course: world
lesson: 130
title: Screen time, wasted time, and putting it all together
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Small daily times add up to huge yearly ones: 2 hours of games a day is 14 hours a week and 730 hours a year, about 30 whole days and nights. Multiply your daily time by 365 to see what a habit really costs.
explain: "Wasting time" means spending it in a way you later wish you hadn't; resting, chilling and playing aren't waste if you chose them. The trouble is that apps are built to keep you going, with autoplay and endless feeds, so "5 minutes" of scrolling quietly becomes 45. A timer beats your feeling of time.
explain: Doing the hard job first, "eating the frog", leaves the fun part free of worry. Grown-ups say "time is money" because many are paid by the hour: at £12 an hour, 3 hours of work is £36. For children, practice "pays" later in skills.
explain: Putting it all together means doing time sums before you act. At 7:40, an 8-minute game and then a 10-minute walk gets you to the stop at 7:58, 3 minutes after the 7:55 bus has gone. As the old saying goes, "time and tide wait for no man": the sea's tide comes in about every 12 and a half hours whether you're ready or not.
fact: 2 hours a day is 2 × 7 = 14 hours a week and 2 × 365 = 730 hours a year, about 30 days.
fact: 6 hours out of 24 is a quarter of a day.
fact: Resting is not wasting time if you chose it; wasting time means spending it in a way you later wish you hadn't.
fact: Apps use autoplay and endless feeds to keep you scrolling, so timers and app limits help.
fact: "Eat the frog" means do the hardest job first.
fact: A week has 7 × 24 = 168 hours; 30 hours of school, 70 of sleep and 2 of football leave 66 hours.
fact: "Time is money" means time is valuable; at £12 an hour, 3 hours of work earns £36.
fact: Leaving at 7:48 with a 10-minute walk, you reach the stop at 7:58, 3 minutes after a 7:55 bus.
fact: A plane leaving London at 10:00 and flying 3 hours lands at 13:00 London time, which is 15:00 in Kyiv, 2 hours ahead.
fact: From 9 December to 4 January is 26 days: 22 to the end of December, then 4 more.
fact: The tide comes in and goes out about twice a day, roughly every 12 and a half hours.
fact: "Time and tide wait for no man" means time keeps going whatever we do.
fact: Football every 3 days from Monday the 1st falls on the 1st, 4th, 7th and so on, on a different weekday each time: the 4th is a Thursday and the 7th a Sunday.
exercise: Josh plays 2 hours every day. How many hours is that in a week? | 14 | 9 | 2 | 24
exercise: How many hours is 2 hours a day for a year of 365 days? | 730 | 365 | 104 | 7,300
exercise: About how many whole days is 730 hours? | About 30 | About 7 | About 73 | About 365
exercise: Ruby watched 6 hours of videos. What part of the 24-hour day is that? | A quarter | A half | A third | A tenth
exercise: When is resting not a waste of time? | When you chose it and it does you good | Never, because resting is always a waste of time | Only when you are ill and stuck in bed all day | Only on Sundays, when the shops are all shut
exercise: Mia meant to scroll for 5 minutes but scrolled for 45. How many extra minutes? | 40 | 50 | 9 | 45
exercise: Why is it hard to stop scrolling? | Apps use autoplay and endless feeds to keep you going | Phones lock your fingers to the screen | Videos get shorter the longer you watch | Your eyes need the bright light from the screen to stay open
exercise: What does "eat the frog" mean? | Do the hardest job first | Eat something gross for a dare | Jump from one job to another | Leave the worst job till last
exercise: How many hours are in a week? | 168 | 24 | 100 | 148
exercise: Ben has 30 hours of school, 70 of sleep and 2 of football in a week. How many hours are left? | 66 | 102 | 168 | 76
exercise: At £12 an hour, how much is 3 hours of work? | £36 | £15 | £4 | £312
exercise: It's 7:40. Leo plays for 8 minutes, then walks for 10. When does he reach the stop? | 7:58 | 7:50 | 7:55 | 8:08
exercise: Eve's plane leaves London at 10:00 and flies 3 hours. Kyiv is 2 hours ahead. What time is it in Kyiv when she lands? | 15:00 | 13:00 | 11:00 | 12:00
exercise: It's 9 December. How many days until 4 January? | 26 | 25 | 22 | 36
exercise: About how often does the tide come in? | About every 12 and a half hours | Once a month | About every hour | Once a year
exercise: Football is every 3 days, starting on Monday the 1st. Which date is the third football day? | The 7th | The 3rd | The 6th | The 9th
exercise: What does "time and tide wait for no man" mean? | Time keeps going, whatever we do | A man called Tide is always late for things | Sailors must never wait for the tide | Clocks stop when the tide comes in
test: Josh: "It's only 2 hours a day, chill." What does that add up to in a year? | About 730 hours, roughly a whole month | About 2 hours in total, since it's only once a day | About 24 hours, which is just one single day | About 365 hours, which is roughly one week
test: Ruby: "I wasted the whole flippin' day!" She watched 6 hours of videos. What's true? | It was a quarter of the day, and rest isn't waste if she chose it | It was the whole 24 hours of the day | It was half the day, and all of it was wasted, because rest is always a waste of time | It was 6 minutes, so it doesn't count
test: Kai's mum: "No screens till your homework's done, sunshine." Kai's finished in 25 minutes: "Done and dusted!" ("Done and dusted" means finished.) What's the idea behind Mum's rule? | Do the less fun job first so worry doesn't spoil the fun | Screens make homework take longer, and that's against the law | Homework is only allowed after it gets dark outside, not before | Games should always come first, so you're relaxed for homework
test: Mia: "I scrolled for 5 mins and it's been 45?! How, though?" Best answer? | Apps keep you going with autoplay and endless feeds; set a timer | Her phone's clock is 40 minutes fast | Time really does go faster on a phone | She must have fallen asleep for 40 minutes without noticing it at all
test: Ben's mate: "School and sleep take your whole week, there's no time left, mate." Check with Ben's plan. | 66 hours are left after school, sleep and football | No hours are left at all | 100 hours are left | Only 6 hours are left
test: Isla: "Time is money? So I can buy sweets with an hour?" What does the saying mean? | Time is valuable; many grown-ups are paid for their time | You can pay for things in the shops with minutes and hours | Clocks are made out of money, so time costs a lot to buy | Money makes time go faster, so rich people get older quicker
test: Leo at 7:40: "One quick game, 8 minutes, then I'll go, sorted." The walk is 10 minutes and the bus comes at 7:55. What happens? | He reaches the stop at 7:58 and misses the bus by 3 minutes | He makes it with 3 minutes to spare | He reaches the stop at exactly 7:55 | He reaches the stop at 7:48
test: Eve's dad: "10 o'clock off, 3 hours flying, so we land at 1, yeah?" They fly London to Kyiv, 2 hours ahead. Fix it. | 1 pm London time is 3 pm in Kyiv | Right, it's 1 pm in Kyiv | It's 11 am in Kyiv | It's 5 pm in Kyiv
test: Tom on 9 December: "Like, 4 weeks to my birthday on 4 Jan, innit?" Exactly how many sleeps is it? | 26 | 28 | 22 | 30
test: Grandad: "Time and tide wait for no man." Ava: "Who's Tide, then?" Best answer? | The tide is the sea coming in and going out; it won't wait, just like time | Tide was a famous sailor who was always late | Tide is the name of a clock in Greenwich | Tide is a man who sells watches
test: Kai's teacher: football every 3 days from Monday the 1st. Kai: "So it's always Mondays, easy." What's right? | No: it lands on a different weekday each time, like Thursday the 4th | Yes: every 3 days is always a Monday | No: it is always on Wednesdays | Yes: but only in the months of the year that have 31 days in them, like May
test: What would happen if Josh cut his games from 2 hours to 1 hour a day for a year? | He'd free about 365 hours, about 15 days | He'd free about 1 hour in total | He'd free about 730 hours | He'd free about 52 hours
exam: Given that an activity occupies two hours daily, determine the aggregate duration over a year of 365 days. | Seven hundred and thirty hours | Three hundred and sixty-five hours | Fourteen hours | Seven thousand three hundred hours
exam: It is given that a week comprises 168 hours, of which 30 are devoted to school, 70 to sleep and 2 to football. Ascertain the number of hours remaining. | Sixty-six | One hundred and two | Seventy-six | Ninety-eight
exam: A flight departs London at 10:00 London time, its duration being three hours; Kyiv is two hours ahead of London. Ascertain the local time of arrival in Kyiv. | 15:00 | 13:00 | 11:00 | 17:00
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the saying "time and tide wait for no man". | Time proceeds irrespective of human readiness or delay | The tides may be postponed at the request of sailors | Time is measured exclusively by the tides | All persons must await the tide before acting
